\section{Which unit a test row is predicted for}{

\code{groups} says how the random effects and the correlation over time enter,
as in \code{\link[=ilm_predict]{ilm_predict()}}. By default it follows the folds:
\itemize{
\item \strong{grouped folds} hold out whole units, so a test row's unit is new to the
training fit, and it is predicted as a new unit (\code{"new"});
\item \strong{random, blocked and rolling folds} keep a unit's other rows in the
training data, so it is predicted as itself (\code{"fitted"}).
}

Random or blocked folds on a model with a correlation over time score
interpolation, not forecasting: a test row sits between training rows of its
own series, so its neighbours' cells inform it. \code{ilm_cv()} warns, and names
rolling-origin folds, which score forecasts.
}

\section{Ex-ante and ex-post}{

Rolling-origin folds score forecasts. A covariate whose future value would
not have been known at the origin, such as the weather on the day forecast,
makes the evaluation \emph{ex-post}: it flatters the model. The folds record which
covariates are known in advance (\code{ilm_folds(known = )}); the result says
which evaluation it is.
}

